# {{project.name}}

Generated by ModelScaffolder. Each schema is an independent slice with its own API and `DbContext`;
all slices share one PostgreSQL database, separated by database schema.

| Project | Contents |
|---|---|
| `{Schema}.Domain` | Entities |
| `{Schema}.Infrastructure` | `{Schema}DbContext`, entity configurations, `IGenericAsyncRepository<T>` via GenericRepository.Services.EFCore |
| `{Schema}.Validation` | ReValidator rules, one `Rules/{entity}-rules.json` per entity (editable without recompiling; restart to apply) |
| `{Schema}.Policies` | Permission claims `{Action}.{Schema}.{Entity}`; RePolicies policies, one `Definitions/{entity}-{action}.policy.json` per policy |
| `{Schema}.Application` | Request/response DTOs, mappings, one service per entity |
| `{Schema}.Api` | Minimal API endpoints; users in `Authentication:Users` in `appsettings.json` |
| `tests/{Schema}/{Schema}.Application.Tests` | xUnit + NSubstitute unit tests for every service |
| `tests/{Schema}/{Schema}.Api.Tests` | HTTP integration tests (real auth, policies, validation, EF) against PostgreSQL in Docker (Testcontainers) |
| `Authentication.Basic` (shared) | HTTP Basic authentication handler, user store options, PBKDF2 `PasswordHash` |

## Run

1. Start PostgreSQL and check `ConnectionStrings:Default` in each `appsettings.json`.
2. Run an API, e.g. `dotnet run --project src/{{#each schemas}}{{#if @first}}{{name}}/{{name}}.Api{{/if}}{{/each}}`. In Development it creates its database schema and tables; use EF migrations beyond prototyping.

## Tests

`dotnet test` runs both test projects per schema. The API tests start a PostgreSQL container, so Docker must be running.
For manual testing, each API has a `{Schema}.Api.http` file (Visual Studio HTTP client) that chains requests through the created ids.

## Authentication and permissions

Every endpoint requires an authenticated user (401 otherwise) and passes RePolicies (403 otherwise). Endpoints are
marked with `WithPolicyModel<TModel>()` and `UseRePolicies()` enforces the policies loaded from `Definitions/*.policy.json`
(edit them next to the API binaries and restart). POST/PUT policies see the request body as `x.Model`; GET/DELETE have
no body, so `x.Model` is null there. By default each policy requires a permission claim:
`Get`, `Create`, `Update` or `Delete` + `.{Schema}.{Entity}`, e.g. `Create.{{#each schemas}}{{#if @first}}{{name}}.{{#each entities}}{{#if @first}}{{name}}{{/if}}{{/each}}{{/if}}{{/each}}`.

The system user `{{project.systemUserName}}` entered when scaffolding has every permission. Its password is stored only as a
PBKDF2 hash. To add users, append entries to `Authentication:Users` (create a hash with `PasswordHash.Create("...")`).
Basic authentication sends the password with every request, so use HTTPS outside local development.

